{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>These are the commandments which YHWH commanded Moses for the children of Israel at Mount Sinai.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: אלה המצות — Rabbi Ila said: if you perform them as ordained, behold they are commandments; and if not, they are not commandments. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Torat Kohanim: אלה המצות — that no prophet is permitted to introduce anything new from now on. אשר צוה ה' את משה — the emissary is worthy of Him who sent him. את משה אל בני ישראל — the emissary is worthy of the one to whom he was sent. אל בני ישראל — the merit of Israel caused it. בהר סיני — for all of them were given at Sinai; thus far in Torat Kohanim. And the intent of this Midrash is that the fact that the book concludes with the expression אלה המצות was to indicate that the commandments are eternal and enduring for all generations, for thus the word אלה is an expression of standing and permanence forever, regarding things whose existence shall not be shaken or falter, in the manner of the verse (Genesis 2:4) אלה תולדות השמים והארץ — which stand forever from the aspect of their creation; and likewise regarding Israel (Numbers 2:32) אלה פקודי בני ישראל לבית אבותם. And this verse informs us who commanded them, and through whom, and to whom, and in what place He spoke with him face to face. He said first אשר צוה ה' to testify to the exaltedness of the Commander, may He be exalted, whose glory our eyes beheld and whose voice from amidst the fire our ears heard. And in order to testify to the truth of the prophecy of the emissary, established in our thought and inscribed in our souls — not by his signs and wonders but by our own testimony: our eyes saw and not a stranger's, and a stranger did not perform it (ולעד לא עשה) — therefore he said את משה. And to testify concerning the people who received the Torah, whose thought preceded before the mountains of El were born, he said אל בני ישראל. And to testify concerning the place in which they were given and in which our thoughts trembled, he mentioned בהר סיני. There every precious thing my eye saw; when I beheld His preciousness, there my soul went forth at His word; He shone from Seir and appeared from Mount Paran; and He came from the myriads of holy ones; there I saw the chariot of Elohim, myriads, thousands of Shin'an; YHWH came from Sinai with holiness (cf. Deuteronomy 33:2; Psalm 68:18). <br><b>Thus concludes the book of Leviticus (ספר ויקרא).</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:14.851239203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}